    "stop_reason": "success",
    "result_text": "`AcceptanceTest` is the only test class, and both tests pass with a clean `BUILD SUCCESS`.\n\n## Summary\n\nI redesigned the owner identity key and reworked duplicate detection around it.\n\n**New identity key** (`Owner.getIdentityKey()`): now the lower-case SHA-256 hex digest of `normalizedTelephone + '|' + lowerEmail + '|' + soundex(lastName)` (was the plaintext `telephone|email|householdId`). This is the single key that drives 409 duplicate detection.\n\n**New `Soundex` utility** (`rest/validation/Soundex.java`): a standard American Soundex encoder (first letter + three digits, H/W transparent, vowels non-coding), shared by the identity key and the soft-match detector so both agree on when two surnames sound alike.\n\n**Soft-match redesign** (`PossibleDuplicateOwnerDetector`): now flags a possible duplicate when `soundex(lastName)` **and** postcode match but the identity key differs \u2014 replacing the old \"same last name + postcode + different telephone\" rule. As a direct consequence, two owners with the same last name and postcode but different telephones now derive different identity keys, so they are created as a soft match rather than rejected with 409.\n\n**Duplicate rejection** (`HouseholdDuplicateValidator`) already keyed off `getIdentityKey()`, so it needed no logic change \u2014 with the new key it's now the sole 409 path; the email-domain blocklist still runs first and deleted owners are still ignored.\n\nI also updated the related Javadoc and the `openapi.yml` descriptions/examples for `identityKey`, `possibleDuplicate`, and `possibleDuplicateOf` to match the new behaviour. `householdId` is still computed and used for household size, membership caps, etc.; it simply no longer participates in the identity key.\n\nEverything compiles and the full suite (`Tests run: 2, Failures: 0, Errors: 0`) is green.",
